%I A000789 M1347 N0516 #52 Nov 10 2026 16:48:43
%S A000789 2,5,8,13,16,21,26,35,38,45,48,57
%N A000789 Maximal order of a triangle-free cyclic graph with no independent set of size n.
%C A000789 Previous name was: Ramsey numbers.
%C A000789 The sequence may be considered as consisting of a special kind of Ramsey numbers. It is related to the ordinary two-color Ramsey numbers R(3,n), given in A000791, by the relation a(n) <= A000791(n)-1 as proved by Kalbfleisch. He also calculated the first eight terms, and noted that the inequality sometimes is strict. The first n for which this happens is n=6.
%C A000789 The terms a(10), a(11) and a(12) were calculated by Harborth and Krause. - _Jörgen Backelin_, Jan 07 2016
%C A000789 a(13) = 57 follows from the circulant Ramsey number R_C(3,13) = 58 established by Coniglio et al. (2026). Independent confirmation: the circulant graph on 57 vertices with distances {1, 5, 8, 12, 14, 18} is triangle-free with independence number 12, and an exhaustive search shows that every triangle-free circulant graph on 58..68 vertices has an independent set of size 13 (only degrees <= 12 need checking, since the neighborhood of a vertex in a triangle-free graph is independent; 1206892 distance sets in 77611 orbits under multiplication by units). Larger orders are excluded by R(3,13) <= 68 (Goedgebeur and Radziszowski). The upper bound a(13) <= 57 also follows from Kuznetsov's enumeration of distance colorings, which include the circulant ones. - _Benjamin Schippers_, Nov 03 2026
%D A000789 H. Harborth, S. Krause: Ramsey Numbers for Circulant Colorings, Congressus Numerantium 161 (2003), pp. 139-150.
%D A000789 N. J. A. Sloane, A Handbook of Integer Sequences, Academic Press, 1973 (includes this sequence).
%D A000789 N. J. A. Sloane and Simon Plouffe, The Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences, Academic Press, 1995 (includes this sequence).
%H A000789 Stefano Coniglio, Fabio Furini, Ivana Ljubić, Pablo San Segundo, Johannes Thüerauf, and Emiliano Traversi, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.18769">An Integer Programming Approach to Compute Lower Bounds for Ramsey Numbers Using Circulant Graphs</a>, arXiv:2608.18769 [math.OC], 2026.
%H A000789 Jan Goedgebeur and Stanisław P. Radziszowski, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.5826">New Computational Upper Bounds for Ramsey Numbers R(3,k)</a>, arXiv:1210.5826 [math.CO], 2012-2013; Electron. J. Combin. 20(1) (2013), #P30.
%H A000789 J. G. Kalbfleisch, <a href="https://doi.org/10.4153/CMB-1965-041-7">Construction of special edge-chromatic graphs</a>, Canad. Math. Bull., 8 (1965), 575-584.
%H A000789 Eugene Kuznetsov, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.07186">Computational lower limits on small Ramsey numbers</a>, arXiv:1505.07186 [cs.DM], 2015-2016.
%e A000789 That a(6) >= 16 is seen from the cyclic (or circulant) graph on 16 vertices, with edges between vertices of index distances 1, 3, or 8, since this cyclic graph indeed is triangle-free and has independence number five, which is less than six.
%e A000789 On the other hand, a(6) < 17, since any triangle free graph with independence number less than six and at least 17 vertices has exactly 17 vertices and cannot be regular, but all cyclic graphs are regular.
%e A000789 Thus, indeed, a(6) = 16.
%Y A000789 Cf. A000791.
%K A000789 nonn,hard,more,changed
%O A000789 2,1
%A A000789 _N. J. A. Sloane_
%E A000789 New title and a(10)-a(12) from by _Jörgen Backelin_, Jan 12 2016
%E A000789 a(13) from  _Benjamin Schippers_, Nov 03 2026
